{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>This time.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this time alone it is that a bone was taken from my bones, but from here onward there will be no need to take a woman from a rib; rather, the creatures will engage in propagation. Thus Rashi, of blessed memory, interpreted." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'a bone from my bones' — the woman is harsh in her nature, because she was created from bone; but the nature of man is agreeable, for he was created from dust, which is agreeable. And because of this, you find concerning man ״וייצר״ — for he was like clay, which is agreeable in the hand of the potter; but concerning the woman, it is said of her ״ויבן״ — like a man who builds a structure with stones, which are hard. From this, Scripture said (Psalms 103:13): ״כרחם אב על בנים״ — it attributed compassion to the father, and it attributed the attribute of judgment to the mother, as it is written (Proverbs 31:1): ״משא אשר יסרתו אמו״. And from within this passage it is clarified that the woman was created in the Garden of Eden, for there she was taken from the ribs of man, a bone from his bones; whereas the man, who was placed in the Garden, was created outside the Garden."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:38.392249687Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}